2. who calls main :::

--> the operating system (OS) doesn’t jump directly to main().
Instead, it loads your executable into memory and starts execution at a special entry point defined by the C runtime startup code, usually called _start.
---> > _start is provided by the C runtime library (libc) — not by your program.
-->> _start calls   __libc_start_main(), which in turn calls your main().

(( OS → _start → __libc_start_main() → main() → return → exit()  ))

--> When your main() function finishes (e.g., return 0;),
__libc_start_main() catches that return value and calls the exit() function .

3.LINKER
linker will link object files and libraries and combined into them an executable file.

Static linking
Copies required library code directly into your executable.
cc main.c -static -o prog
Dynamic linking
Keeps library code in shared .so files (loaded at runtime).
cc main.c -o prog


4. LOADER :: The loader is part of the operating system — not part of the compiler toolchain.
->It’s responsible for loading your program (the executable file produced by the linker) into memory and preparing it to run.

Ex: ./prog


the loader:
    1. Reads the ELF header of prog.
    2. Maps your program’s segments (text, data, bss) into memory.
    3. Loads required shared libraries (e.g., libc.so.6, libm.so.6).
    4. Performs dynamic relocation (resolving symbols between libraries).
    5. Sets up the stack and environment.
    6. Jumps to the program’s entry point (_start).
So the loader executes after the linker has produced the executable.
